I must admit I haven't seen very much of TNG, least of all the Borgs (being
Geographically Challenged here in Sweden), so much of my information is based
on what I have read on the Net. But that won't stop me... :-)

>The Borgs lack individuality. A single Borg unit can function 
>meaningfully only in the beehive which comprises other borgs. They have a 
>collective consciousness. The borg units think together, their primordial 
>thoughts are shared amongst the other units, as must be their higher 
>levels of reasoning (?...?)

Note the lack of diversity. As far as I understand, each unit is
interchangeable with every other, and their society/consciousness seems to
be fairly monolithic. This might correspond to a single posthumanoid
entity rather than a society (although it may have evolved from a
society), although the difference between individuals and collectives in
the posthuman era will probably be smaller than now. 

Actually, posthumanoid entities will probably often appear as Borg-like
societies to pre-singularity humans, consisting of many interchangeable
units apparently on the human scale (and other scaled), controlled by an
Overmind. However, the units are more to be seen as cells or organs in a
single being than independent beings (although there might be some use for
units capable of independent action). However, I doubt a posthumanoid
society/entity will be very uniform. Since there is strength in diversity,
it would be much more reasonable to employ very different units and
methods for different problems, making a real Borg very heterogenous. The
same is true for the Overmind, since its probably more efficient to
organize mental processes in a fractal hierarchy than have a single mind. 

