From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Thu, 3 Nov 1994 09:49:03 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-58] CONSPIRACY: Is HotWired ashamed of its advertising?

FORWARD AND REPOST THIS MESSAGE AS FAR AND WIDE AS YOU CAN...

It took me about five minutes of using HotWired to figure out a number of
interrelated mysteries:

1) Why Mosaic Communications got such a big plug in WiReD 2.10;

2) Why HotWired won't admit MacWeb users;

3) Why Netscape (by Mosaic Communications) doesn't let you selectively load
inline images.

HotWired is Prodigy turned loose on the Net.  As they tell you in the
intro, HotWired is supported by advertisers; the advertising is displayed
at the top of each screen as an inline image (which is also a live link to
more detailed adverts for that particular sponsor).

The MacWeb WWW browser (by Microelectronics Computer & Technology
Corporation) lets you load inline images selectively, by clicking on an
icon placeholder in the text.  This lets SLIP and other narrowband users
choose how to spend their precious bandwidth.  (I haven't used NCSA Mosaic
for the Macintosh or any other Mac browsers yet, so I don't know where they
stand on this feature.)

Netscape, however, has a single button for loading all inline images in a
Web page.  You can't choose to just load some images and not others; you
have to swallow the whole graphical firehose.  (The "Reload" feature also
forces loading of all images, even if you never requested images.  Hmmm...)

NOTA BENE:

1) I recognize the vital economic function of advertising.  I enjoy not
having to pay $7.50 for today's Mercury News.  I appreciate the risk an
advertiser takes in spending advertising dollars on a medium that may or
may not reach the advertiser's target audience (I seldom buy a newspaper,
so money spent to advertise in the Merc is wasted on me).

2) I recognize the need to commercialize the Internet; it has already been
"free" (see below) to too many people for so long that the Great Unwashed
are starting to demand that you and I pay for terminals for the homeless in
the public library.

3) I have nothing against logrolling ^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H^H strategic
partnerships.  I know the computing industry runs on them.  "One hand
washes the other" is good clean fun IF it's done in the open.


What I object to is SNEAKINESS and HYPOCRISY.  This is from the blurb you
get by e-mail when you register on HotWired:

>    Isn't Advertising Anathema on the Net?
>    The Net community does indeed react negatively to invasive
>    advertising - the kind of spamming conducted recently by the
>    Arizona lawyers Canter and Siegel, which elicited a massive
>    rejection by the Net's immune system. The advertising on
>    HotWired is the opposite of invasive.
>
>    Each advertiser is accessible only through a single discreet
>    banner at the head of a content section. Most advertising is 90
>    percent persuasion and 10 percent information; advertising on
>    HotWired reverses this ratio. And the privacy of members is
>    guaranteed by HotWired's unqualified commitment to never
>    divulge a member's personal information to advertisers.
     [...]
>    What Does HotWired Cost?
>    HotWired is free to members. HotWired's revenue model is
>    similar to broadcast media - content supported by sponsors.

     [And we all know how well the broadcast media turned out -- dV/dt]

>    HotWired's sponsors are some of the bluest chip advertisers in
>    America, including IBM, AT+T, Volvo, Sprint, MCI, Zima
>    (Coors), Internet Shopping Network (Home Shopping
>    Network), Club Med, etc.

The hypocrisy is in making the statement that "the advertising on HotWired
is the opposite of invasive," then undergoing significant (unpublicized)
machinations to ensure that EVERY USER MUST DOWNLOAD EVERY ADVERTISING
IMAGE.

Forced gang-loading of all inline images in a Web document is a serious
misfeature of a Web browser, advertising or no.  Internet users riding
modems still outnumber those with direct connect; and many of those users
pay for connect time by the minute.  That the programmer, rather than the
user, gets to decide which inline images are worth spending bandwidth to
retrieve, and that the decision that all images are of equal worth is
_hardwired_ (if you'll pardon the expression) into the program, is
domineering and, in my opinion as an interface designer, inexcusable.

If my suspicions are correct, wouldn't the honest and forthright thing to
say have been:

        What does HotWired cost?
        HotWired is free to members.  HotWired's revenue model is
        similar to broadcast media - content supported by sponsors.
        So, to ensure that we provide good value to our sponsors,
        we've taken steps to ensure that HotWired users must view
        the advertisements on the system.

I would have no problem with such a statement -- it recognizes the economic
realities of trying to jump-start a new medium in competition with "free"
(advertiser-sponsored) television and radio.  But the appearance of a
sneaky attempt to force-feed advertising to users and _cover it up_ as if
it were shameful instead of an economic necessity makes me queasy...
especially when HotWired purports to look down its nose at the competition:

>    [...] It's not an online service like Prodigy
>    or AOL (now rendered obsolete by the explosion of interest in
>    the Internet and the development of the Web and graphical
>    browsers).

... so long as the graphical browsers give US the same control over what
you see...

By the way, the HotWired management make another statement with respect to
the broadcast media:

>    Today is like 1948; a new medium has reached critical mass.
>    We're trying to help define the future of that medium before
>    it ends up like television.

I submit that choosing the same economic model as television will
inevitably lead to the same crap-dominated content as television (with the
proviso that, since HotWired isn't part of a state-enforced oligopoly like
broadcast TV, the chance exists that other services with the pay-per-view
economic model may get the chance to arise).  Is it possible that America's
advertisers don't want the pay-per-view economic model to succeed?

These developments are particularly sinister in light of the proposed
"National Information Infrastructure" program to socialize the Net.  Will a
government-dictated economic model of "free" (advertiser-funded) access for
all ensure that the Internet becomes, like TV and radio, a
government-controlled oligopoly where individuals with dissenting views but
no huge advertising budget cannot get an audience?

Many people are promoting the idea that what makes the Net great is that
it's mostly "free" -- which really means that the accounts of most users
are paid for by someone else (their employer, their university).  This is a
dangerous fallacy; what really makes the Net great is that it's the first
medium in the history of communications that is _any-to-many_ -- a college
freshman at Podunk State can post to alt.whatever and communicate with a
narrowcatch audience that spans the globe.  The Internet, unlike any other
medium in existence, gives the individual the power to reach a widespread
audience -- on his or her own nickel.  My commercial Internet account, at
$17.50 a month, gives me the power to get my words to YOU, wherever you are
reading this, without the need to be approved by a government censor or a
"network standards and practices" operative with the FCC looking over his
shoulder.

It's a calamity that English uses the same word "free" to mean both "(1) at
no cost" and "(2) not subject to regulation or domination".  If individuals
are to regain power over their own lives, THE NET MUST REMAIN FREE(2).  And
the way to make the net free(2) is to keep it from becoming free(1) -- to
continue to ensure that your words -- your free(2)dom to speak -- are paid
for and owned by YOU, and not by some corporate or governmental "sponsor".


[Yes, large parts of the present Net are tax-funded.  Suppose it's 50%.  My
$17.50 per month would double.  I'd gladly pay it to keep the net free(2).]

Maybe HotWired should be ashamed of its advertising, after all.  Any
competent economist can tell you what will happen to the quality of content
in a medium where those who pay the bills are "pushing" the information as
"sponsors", not "pulling" it as users.  Crap (remember Sturgeon's Law) that
appeals to the lowest common denominator grabs all the advertisers (because
it has all the audience), and quality material (like the content of
HotWired, which is actually quite good) starves, unable to compete with the
free junk.  Advertisers also shy away from sponsoring anything that might
offend anybody anywhere, so controversy, diversity, dissent, and choice all
go away, and the Net becomes another "vast wasteland" populated by "Green
Acres" and "COPS".  This becomes a real danger when the introduction of new
providers to the Net is artificially restricted by some form of limit to
entry, such as Al Gore's expressed desire to make Net providers screen
their content for "suitability for children", introducing the Net
equivalent of Hollywood's Hays Office.

I hope to hell I'm wrong, and that HotWired and Mosaic Communications are
not trying to pull a fast one on their users.  It would be easy enough to
prove.  Mosaic Communications could add to V1.0 of Netscape the feature of
selective downloading of inline images.  HotWired could admit browsers
(like MacWeb) that allow selective downloading.

Or they could come out and admit that they want you to be a passive
consumer and have taken steps to ensure it.  Either route would be a noble
way to resolve the controversy, IMHO; again, I don't object to HotWired
users being forced to view the ads that pay for the service, I just want
them to be aware that it's happening.

If I'm wrong, and the parties involved choose to prove it in one of the
ways I describe, I'll happily recant my suspicions in whatever forum they
appear.  Otherwise, I have to conclude that something shady is going on
under the table -- a business practice for which there is no NEED to be
covert, that shouldn't even be considered shameful.  In the meantime, I
strongly recommend that you think twice about HotWired and Netscape, and
stand up to keep the Net user-sponsored -- unless you _really_ want the Net
to become a take-it-or-leave-it medium like television.
